A||N||8||burdell1||Good Glue?||||1||
Z||000000||burdell1||01-16-2000||01:23 PM||||My center hub (the black vinyl part around the heater and radio) is coming loose and was wondering what is a good glue for this? also, what is a good way to glue it since it is hard to keep it down.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||01-16-2000||01:57 PM||||I used Plastic Epoxy on mine and it has held for almost three years so far.  I dug out the old foam where it had separated from the vinyl cover material.  I mixed some of the Epoxy (got it at Walmart in a two part syringe type applicator) and poured it into the void between the cover material and the plastic frame and clamped a couple of small sticks (like popsicle sticks) over the repair to hold it in place while the glue cured.  I used wax paper between the sticks and the vinyl to keep the sticks from getting glued to the part.<P>Since I had to depend on gravity to keep the uncured glue from oozing out I only did one side at a time and positioned the part such that the glue stayed where I wanted it.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||01-16-2000||05:03 PM||||I used window sealing "urethane" to hold my console pieces together. Like Carrolles said, be sure to remove as much loose foam as you can to make sure the glue takes a good bite. If you are not familiar with urethane, you can glue dirt together with it and make it stick! But, if you get it on your skin, it smears really really bad and it pretty hard to get off. Water and humidity make it dry, so washing it off your hands won't work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||01-16-2000||06:02 PM||||I've tried all kinds of glue - soft, hard, low temp, high temp but things always come loose again after a while because of temperature changes inside the car. I found that white silicone glues stuff like nothing else, it'll never come loose by itself, yet it is easily removed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||01-17-2000||10:45 AM||||Try crazy glue, you will love it. mine has been on for two years and still holding. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||01-17-2000||12:42 PM||||Krazy glue is good because it doesn't need any time to set-up.  I just pour a little on and squeeze till it holds itself.  My interior was really coming apart too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||01-17-2000||12:42 PM||||Try Super Glue Gel. Works on everything. The gel fills gaps and cracks.||||reg||14
Z||000007||mutfish||01-17-2000||09:26 PM||||I use cyanoacrylate glue (gap filling type) made by Great Planes, It's available at hobbie shops, it is used in radio control model building. I think it is a lot better than the cheaper "super" type glues.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||01-17-2000||09:39 PM||||As long as you're at the Hobby Shop getting high quality super glue, go ahead and get some Zip-Kicker. You spray it on any cyanoacrylate (super) glue and it forces it to dry INSTANTLY! Be warned, large amounts of glue and Zip-Kicker get really HOT and it will smoke. But, it beats having to hold stuff together and blowing it trying to make it dry.||||reg||1
